import Mathlib
import AssemblyP1.FiniteBayesianSideInformation
import AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationOrder

/-!
# Source-defined property promises and the weakest sufficient one (#272)

This module discharges one of the concrete obligations left OPEN by the merged
`#272` order-theoretic modules:

* `AssemblyP1.FiniteBayesianSideInformation` (`b0a6e9f`, PR #195) fixed the exact
  posterior semantics `π g · p_g(r) · 1_E(g,r)` and the structural predicate
  `Sufficient`.
* `AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationOrder` (`c312cbbc`, PR #204) reduced, for any
  equivalence-closed admissible family `Adm`, the question "does a weakest
  (least informative) sufficient promise exist?" to the single obligation
  `Adm (unionAdmissible Adm π p orbit) ∧ Sufficient (unionAdmissible Adm π p orbit)`
  (`weakest_admissible_iff_union`), and observed (§6) that the *source-defined
  bridging subfamily* is covered by neither the positive read-only result nor the
  negative unrestricted result.

The present module resolves that obligation for the **source-defined property
family**: promises that reveal a property of the *candidate genome* and not of
the observed read — the abstract form of a side channel that reports a
source-model property of the true genome. This family generalizes the
deterministic truth-only label family of `AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationColoring`
(PR #164).

## Result

| promise family | weakest sufficient promise | existence |
|---|---|---|
| read-only `AdmReadOnly` (merged) | `Unambiguous`, unique | always |
| source property (truth-only) `AdmTruthOnly` | the vacuous promise `True`, unique up to equivalence | **iff** the read-only posterior is already certain, i.e. `∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r` |
| arbitrary truth-dependent (merged `NoWeakest`) | none generically | fails on any ambiguous model |

The engine is the order-theoretic observation that a family able to name an
individual candidate always has admissible union `True`:

* `unionAdmissible_eq_true_of_singletons` — if `Adm` contains every singleton
  truth-promise `g = g₀`, then its union of admissible sufficient promises is
  the constant-`True` promise.
* `weakest_admissible_iff_sufficient_true` — for such an equivalence-closed
  family (that also contains `True`), a weakest admissible sufficient promise
  exists **iff** `Sufficient (fun _ _ => True)`.
* `weakest_truthOnly_iff_readonly_certain` — specialized to `AdmTruthOnly`, this
  is **iff** `∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r`.
* `true_isWeakest_truthOnly`, `weakest_truthOnly_unique` — when it exists, the
  weakest source-property sufficient promise is `True`, unique up to
  equivalence.
* `AmbiguousRead.no_weakest`, `AmbiguousRead.counterexample` — an explicit
  finite model (two candidates, one ambiguous read) on which the source-property
  family has **no** weakest sufficient promise, because the union `True` is not
  sufficient. This is a genuine counterexample to existence, not a failure of a
  proof attempt.
* `empty_isStrongestSufficient`, `strongest_unique` — the opposite extreme (the
  *strongest*, i.e. least event) is the empty promise, which always exists and is
  unique up to equivalence. This separates "minimum" (always exists, trivial)
  from "weakest" (the question studied here).

Nothing here asserts the external Shomorony--Kim--Courtade--Tse Not-So-Greedy
theorem, and nothing here decides the ordering of the source predicate `I_s`
against `Unambiguous`. The fully read-dependent source-defined bridging
subfamily generated by `I_s` still needs the literal read-string adapter and
remains OPEN. No `axiom`, `sorry`, or `admit` is introduced; the axiom audit is
`AssemblyP1/FiniteSideInformationSourcePromiseAudit.lean`.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false

namespace AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationSourcePromise

open AssemblyP1.FiniteBayesianSideInformation
open AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationOrder

universe u v w

/-! ## 1. The source-property (truth-only) promise family -/

/-- A promise is a **source property** (truth-only) when it does not depend on
the observed read: the side channel reports a property of the true candidate
genome, not of the read. This is the family of source-defined property promises;
it generalizes the deterministic truth-only label family of
`AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationColoring` (PR #164). -/
def AdmTruthOnly {Genome : Type u} {Obs : Type v} (E : Genome → Obs → Prop) : Prop :=
  ∀ g r r', E g r ↔ E g r'

/-- The source-property family is closed under pointwise logical equivalence. -/
theorem admTruthOnly_congr {Genome : Type u} {Obs : Type v}
    {E E' : Genome → Obs → Prop} (heq : ∀ g r, E g r ↔ E' g r)
    (hE : AdmTruthOnly E) : AdmTruthOnly E' :=
  fun g r r' => (heq g r).symm.trans ((hE g r r').trans (heq g r'))

/-- Every singleton truth-promise is a source property. -/
theorem admTruthOnly_singleton {Genome : Type u} {Obs : Type v} (g₀ : Genome) :
    AdmTruthOnly (fun g (_ : Obs) => g = g₀) :=
  fun _ _ _ => Iff.rfl

/-- The vacuous promise is a source property. -/
theorem admTruthOnly_true {Genome : Type u} {Obs : Type v} :
    AdmTruthOnly (fun (_ : Genome) (_ : Obs) => True) :=
  fun _ _ _ => Iff.rfl

/-! ## 2. The singleton dichotomy -/

section General

variable {Genome : Type u} {Obs : Type v} {Θ : Type w}
variable [Fintype Genome] [Fintype Obs] [Fintype Θ] [DecidableEq Θ]

/-- The singleton truth-promise `g = g₀` is sufficient: its positive-mass fibre
is a single candidate, hence a single orbit. -/
theorem singleton_sufficient (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) (g₀ : Genome) :
    Sufficient π p orbit (fun g _ => g = g₀) := by
  intro g r _ _ hg h _ _ hh
  exact congrArg orbit (hh.trans hg.symm)

/-- **Union of a singleton-containing family is vacuous.**  If an admissible
family `Adm` contains every singleton truth-promise `g = g₀`, then the union of
all admissible sufficient promises is the constant-`True` promise: every
candidate `g` is named by its own sufficient singleton. -/
theorem unionAdmissible_eq_true_of_singletons
    (Adm : (Genome → Obs → Prop) → Prop)
    (hsingle : ∀ g₀, Adm (fun g _ => g = g₀))
    (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ) (orbit : Genome → Θ) :
    ∀ g r, unionAdmissible Adm π p orbit g r ↔ True := by
  intro g r
  constructor
  · intro _; trivial
  · intro _
    exact ⟨fun g' _ => g' = g, hsingle g, singleton_sufficient π p orbit g, rfl⟩

/-- **Existence criterion for singleton-containing families.**  For an
equivalence-closed admissible family `Adm` that contains every singleton
truth-promise and the vacuous promise, a weakest admissible sufficient promise
exists **iff** the vacuous promise is itself sufficient — i.e. iff no side
information is needed at all.  This is `weakest_admissible_iff_union` combined
with the vacuous union of `unionAdmissible_eq_true_of_singletons`. -/
theorem weakest_admissible_iff_sufficient_true
    (Adm : (Genome → Obs → Prop) → Prop)
    (hAdm_congr : ∀ {E E' : Genome → Obs → Prop},
      (∀ g r, E g r ↔ E' g r) → Adm E → Adm E')
    (hsingle : ∀ g₀, Adm (fun g _ => g = g₀))
    (hTrue : Adm (fun _ _ => True))
    (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ) (orbit : Genome → Θ) :
    (∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible Adm π p orbit W) ↔
      Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) := by
  have heq : ∀ g r,
      unionAdmissible Adm π p orbit g r ↔ (fun _ _ => True) g r :=
    fun g r => unionAdmissible_eq_true_of_singletons Adm hsingle π p orbit g r
  have hsu : Sufficient π p orbit (unionAdmissible Adm π p orbit) ↔
      Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) :=
    sufficient_congr π p orbit heq
  have hadm : Adm (unionAdmissible Adm π p orbit) ↔ Adm (fun _ _ => True) :=
    ⟨fun h => hAdm_congr heq h,
      fun h => hAdm_congr (fun g r => (heq g r).symm) h⟩
  have hcrit := weakest_admissible_iff_union Adm hAdm_congr π p orbit
  constructor
  · rintro hW
    exact hsu.mp (hcrit.mpr hW).2
  · intro hS
    exact hcrit.mp ⟨hadm.mpr hTrue, hsu.mpr hS⟩

/-- **Existence criterion for the source-property family.**  A weakest
sufficient source-property promise exists **iff** the vacuous promise is
sufficient. -/
theorem weakest_truthOnly_iff (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) :
    (∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W) ↔
      Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) :=
  weakest_admissible_iff_sufficient_true AdmTruthOnly admTruthOnly_congr
    admTruthOnly_singleton admTruthOnly_true π p orbit

/-- The vacuous promise is sufficient **iff** every read is unambiguous. -/
theorem sufficient_true_iff_unambiguous (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) :
    Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) ↔ ∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r := by
  constructor
  · intro hS r g h hπg hpg hπh hph
    exact hS g r hπg hpg trivial h hπh hph trivial
  · intro hU g r hπg hpg _ h hπh hph _
    exact hU r g h hπg hpg hπh hph

/-- **Manuscript-ready criterion.**  A weakest sufficient source-property
promise exists **iff** the read-only posterior is already certain at every
positive-probability read (`∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r`).  In particular, any
genuinely ambiguous read destroys existence. -/
theorem weakest_truthOnly_iff_readonly_certain (π : Genome → ℝ)
    (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ) (orbit : Genome → Θ) :
    (∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W) ↔
      ∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r :=
  (weakest_truthOnly_iff π p orbit).trans (sufficient_true_iff_unambiguous π p orbit)

/-- The positive direction of the criterion: when every read is unambiguous, a
weakest sufficient source-property promise exists. -/
theorem weakest_truthOnly_exists_of_readonly_certain (π : Genome → ℝ)
    (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ) (orbit : Genome → Θ)
    (h : ∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r) :
    ∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W :=
  (weakest_truthOnly_iff_readonly_certain π p orbit).mpr h

/-- When the vacuous promise is sufficient, it is the weakest sufficient
source-property promise: it is a source property, sufficient, and implied by
every source-property promise. -/
theorem true_isWeakest_truthOnly (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) (hS : Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True)) :
    IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) :=
  ⟨admTruthOnly_true, hS, fun _ _ _ _ _ _ => trivial⟩

/-- **Uniqueness.**  Any weakest sufficient source-property promise is logically
equivalent to the vacuous promise `True`. -/
theorem weakest_truthOnly_unique (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) (hS : Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True))
    {W : Genome → Obs → Prop} (hW : IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W) :
    ∀ g r, W g r ↔ True :=
  weakest_admissible_unique AdmTruthOnly π p orbit hW
    (true_isWeakest_truthOnly π p orbit hS)

/-! ## 3. Minimum versus weakest: the strongest sufficient promise -/

/-- `W` is the **strongest** (most informative, least event) sufficient promise:
sufficient, and implying every sufficient promise. -/
def IsStrongestSufficient (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) (W : Genome → Obs → Prop) : Prop :=
  Sufficient π p orbit W ∧ ∀ E, Sufficient π p orbit E → Stronger W E

/-- The empty promise is always the strongest sufficient promise.  This is the
intrinsic *minimum* (least event) and always exists, in contrast with the
*weakest* (largest event) promise studied above. -/
theorem empty_isStrongestSufficient (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) :
    IsStrongestSufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => False) :=
  ⟨sufficient_false π p orbit, fun _ _ _ _ h => h.elim⟩

/-- The strongest sufficient promise is unique up to logical equivalence. -/
theorem strongest_unique (π : Genome → ℝ) (p : Genome → Obs → ℝ)
    (orbit : Genome → Θ) {W₁ W₂ : Genome → Obs → Prop}
    (h₁ : IsStrongestSufficient π p orbit W₁)
    (h₂ : IsStrongestSufficient π p orbit W₂) :
    ∀ g r, W₁ g r ↔ W₂ g r :=
  stronger_antisymm (h₁.2 W₂ h₂.1) (h₂.2 W₁ h₁.1)

end General

/-! ## 4. Explicit counterexample: no weakest source-property promise

On the merged two-candidate, one-read model (`TwoCandidate` in
`AssemblyP1.FiniteBayesianSideInformation`), read `0` supports both candidates in
different orbits, so the vacuous promise is not sufficient.  By
`weakest_truthOnly_iff`, the source-property family therefore has **no** weakest
sufficient promise, and the union of its sufficient promises (which is `True`) is
not sufficient. -/

namespace AmbiguousRead

open AssemblyP1.FiniteBayesianSideInformation.TwoCandidate

/-- The vacuous promise is not sufficient: the single read is ambiguous. -/
theorem not_sufficient_true :
    ¬ Sufficient π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) := by
  intro hS
  have h := hS (0 : Fin 2) (0 : Fin 1) (by norm_num [π]) (by norm_num [p]) trivial
    (1 : Fin 2) (by norm_num [π]) (by norm_num [p]) trivial
  have h10 : (1 : Fin 2) = 0 := h
  exact (by decide : (1 : Fin 2) ≠ 0) h10

/-- The union of all sufficient source-property promises is the vacuous promise
`True`. -/
theorem union_eq_true :
    ∀ g r, unionAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit g r ↔ True :=
  unionAdmissible_eq_true_of_singletons AdmTruthOnly admTruthOnly_singleton π p orbit

/-- That union is **not** sufficient: it fails at the ambiguous read. -/
theorem union_not_sufficient :
    ¬ Sufficient π p orbit (unionAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit) := by
  intro h
  exact not_sufficient_true
    ((sufficient_congr π p orbit (fun g r => union_eq_true g r)).mp h)

/-- **No weakest sufficient source-property promise exists.** -/
theorem no_weakest :
    ¬ ∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W := by
  intro h
  exact not_sufficient_true ((weakest_truthOnly_iff π p orbit).mp h)

/-- The combined counterexample bundle: the source-property union is `True`, that
union is insufficient, and no weakest sufficient source-property promise
exists. -/
theorem counterexample :
    (∀ g r, unionAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit g r ↔ True) ∧
      ¬ Sufficient π p orbit (unionAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit) ∧
      ¬ ∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W :=
  ⟨union_eq_true, union_not_sufficient, no_weakest⟩

end AmbiguousRead

/-! ## 5. Positive example: a weakest source-property promise does exist

A two-candidate, two-read model in which each read is produced by exactly one
candidate, so every read is unambiguous.  By
`weakest_truthOnly_exists_of_readonly_certain` the source-property family does
have a weakest sufficient promise, namely the vacuous promise `True`. -/

namespace UnambiguousExample

/-- Distinct orbits (identity). -/
def orbit : Fin 2 → Fin 2 := id

/-- Equal prior on the two candidates. -/
noncomputable def π : Fin 2 → ℝ := fun _ => 1 / 2

/-- Read `r` is produced with probability `1` by candidate `r` and `0` by the
other candidate. -/
def p : Fin 2 → Fin 2 → ℝ := fun g r => if g = r then 1 else 0

/-- Candidate `g` has positive likelihood at read `r` exactly when `g = r`. -/
theorem p_pos_iff (g r : Fin 2) : 0 < p g r ↔ g = r := by
  by_cases h : g = r <;> simp [p, h]

/-- Every read is unambiguous: read `r` supports only candidate `r`. -/
theorem unambiguous : ∀ r, Unambiguous π p orbit r := by
  intro r g h _ hpg _ hph
  have hg : g = r := (p_pos_iff g r).mp hpg
  have hh : h = r := (p_pos_iff h r).mp hph
  rw [hg, hh]

/-- A weakest sufficient source-property promise exists. -/
theorem weakest_exists :
    ∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W :=
  weakest_truthOnly_exists_of_readonly_certain π p orbit unambiguous

/-- The weakest sufficient source-property promise is the vacuous promise. -/
theorem true_is_weakest :
    IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) :=
  true_isWeakest_truthOnly π p orbit
    ((sufficient_true_iff_unambiguous π p orbit).mpr unambiguous)

/-- Existence and identification together. -/
theorem weakest_exists_and_is_true :
    (∃ W, IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit W) ∧
      IsWeakestAdmissible AdmTruthOnly π p orbit (fun _ _ => True) :=
  ⟨weakest_exists, true_is_weakest⟩

end UnambiguousExample

end AssemblyP1.FiniteSideInformationSourcePromise
